{ignore}
<p><code>{trans 'cart.title'}</code> looks the key up in the translation catalog of the
request's locale and writes its text. A catalog is a flat JSON object in
<code>_lang/&lt;locale&gt;.json</code> - <code>_lang/en.json</code>, <code>_lang/nl.json</code>.
The catalogs are looked up from the page's directory up to the application root and then
<code>_common</code>, the most specific winning; for a locale <code>nl_NL</code> the files
<code>nl_NL.json</code>, <code>nl-NL.json</code> and <code>nl.json</code> all count. A key no
catalog knows is written as the key itself, so a missing translation shows rather than
disappearing.</p>

<p>The locale is <code>$padLocale</code>. With <code>$padLocales</code> listing several, a
<code>?lang=</code> value, the <code>padLang</code> cookie or the browser's
<code>Accept-Language</code> picks among them.</p>

<h3>Plurals and substitutions</h3>
<p>A text with forms separated by <code>|</code> is plural: two forms are one and other, three
are zero, one and other. <code>count=</code> picks the form and replaces <code>%d</code> in it.
Every other <code>name=value</code> replaces <code>:name</code> in the text. The items are
read raw, as <a href="?tag/attrs"><code>{attrs}</code></a> reads them, so a substitution may be
called anything.</p>

<h3>Escaping</h3>
<p>The catalog's text is the application's own and is written as it stands, markup included.
A substitution is data: it is written as <code>{$x}</code> writes a field, sanitized - and so
is a key that no catalog knows. The <code>trans</code> pipe does the same for a value:
<code>{echo 'cart.title' | trans}</code>. A <code>{trans}</code> without a key is a strict
error.</p>
{/ignore}
